# A Prometheus call with the wrong label count aborts the process, and nothing read it (2026-10-04)

`scripts/ci/check-metric-labels.py` is a new gate. It pairs every Prometheus label-value
call with the labels the metric it names was registered with, and it fails when the two
disagree or when a registration would be rejected outright. This log records why the
repository needs it, which is that the mistake it catches aborts a process and neither the
compiler, nor the panic census, nor a test suite reliably sees it. Everything below was run
on the working tree at `45628088`; the source mutation in section 2 is undone in section 4
and verified undone.

== 0. The premise, read out of the dependency rather than out of memory

Two lines of `prometheus` decide the whole argument. The version is not a guess: `Cargo.lock`
pins `prometheus` 0.14.0, and a fixture asserts that the version the gate's docstring cites
is the version the lockfile names.

    $ grep -n 'pub fn with_label_values\|self.get_metric_with_label_values(vals).unwrap()\|if vals.len() != self.desc.variable_labels.len()' ~/.cargo/registry/src/*/prometheus-0.14.0/src/vec.rs
        118:        if vals.len() != self.desc.variable_labels.len() {
        292:    pub fn with_label_values<V>(&self, vals: &[V]) -> T::M
        296:        self.get_metric_with_label_values(vals).unwrap()

The lookup a call site writes, `src/vec.rs:292`:

    pub fn with_label_values<V>(&self, vals: &[V]) -> T::M
    where
        V: AsRef<str> + std::fmt::Debug,
    {
        self.get_metric_with_label_values(vals).unwrap()
    }

The check that fails first, inside `hash_label_values`, `src/vec.rs:118`:

    if vals.len() != self.desc.variable_labels.len() {
        return Err(Error::InconsistentCardinality {
            expect: self.desc.variable_labels.len(),
            got: vals.len(),
        });
    }

So `with_label_values(&[..])` with one entry too many is an `unwrap` on an `Err`, on the
thread that happens to record the metric. `remove_label_values` and `delete_label_values`
return the same `Err` instead of panicking, which is the quieter half of the problem: the
child metric is never removed and nothing is printed.

== 1. What the tree looks like to the check

    $ python3 scripts/ci/check-metric-labels.py --print
        metric labels hold: 83 registrations, 155 label-value call sites, 0 recorded

83 registrations, of which 82 go through a `register_*!` macro and one is built by hand
(`IntCounterVec::new(Opts::new(..), &[..])` in
`crates/common/xai-computer-hub-sdk/src/metric_donate.rs`). 155 label-value call sites, in
15 files across three crates:

    101  in production code
     54  in test files (handle_tests.rs, hub_server_tests.rs)

The production calls are not on paths a reader can assume a test walks:

     9  crates/codegen/xai-grok-workspace-daemon/src/daemonize.rs
     5  crates/codegen/xai-grok-workspace-daemon/src/preview_supervisor.rs
    26  crates/codegen/xai-grok-workspace/src/handle.rs
     7  crates/codegen/xai-grok-workspace/src/hub_auth/proactive.rs
     9  crates/codegen/xai-grok-workspace/src/hub_server.rs
     3  crates/codegen/xai-grok-workspace/src/permission/hub_permission.rs
    10  crates/codegen/xai-grok-workspace/src/recovery.rs
     3  crates/codegen/xai-grok-workspace/src/session/checkpoint.rs
     1  crates/codegen/xai-grok-workspace/src/session/mod.rs
     8  crates/codegen/xai-grok-workspace/src/session/swap_policy.rs
     8  crates/codegen/xai-grok-workspace/src/upload/mod.rs
     1  crates/common/xai-computer-hub-sdk/src/metric_donate.rs
    11  crates/common/xai-computer-hub-sdk/src/metrics.rs

Whether a given call is reached by a test is a coverage question, and the gate deliberately
does not take it on. What it does instead is judge all 155 statically, in 11 seconds, in
every leg, including the legs that compile nothing.

Two measurements behind the "not covered" list in the docstring, so the list is a
measurement and not a hedge. The other unwrapping form, `with(&HashMap)`, shares its name
with `Cell::with`, `RefCell::with` and this repository's lock helpers, and 244 lines call
`.with(`. Sorting those by whether the file also declares a metric vector leaves exactly two,
both in `crates/codegen/xai-grok-workspace/src/upload/mod.rs`, at lines 558 and 613, and both
are `tracing_subscriber::registry().with(layer)`. No metric here is looked up through `with`
today. On the registration side, all 82 macro calls consume their `Result` inside a
`LazyLock`, 36 by `unwrap` and 46 by `expect`; dropping the `Result` instead would already be
a clippy error, because `Result` is `#[must_use]` and CI runs
`cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets --locked -- -D warnings`.

== 2. One real call, broken on purpose

The claim to test is not "the gate reports what it is told to report", which fixtures cover,
but "a wrong label count is real, and the tools we already run miss it". The mutation is one
extra entry in the array of a production call, `observe_startup_stage` in
`crates/codegen/xai-grok-workspace/src/handle.rs:84`, which records workspace startup stage
timings and has six call sites in that file, on the recovery, catalog, hub-connect and
time-to-ready paths:

    -        .with_label_values(&[stage, outcome])
    +        .with_label_values(&[stage, outcome, "extra"])

Four commands, same tree, in this order.

    $ python3 scripts/ci/check-metric-labels.py
        crates/codegen/xai-grok-workspace/src/handle.rs:85: arity: STARTUP_STAGE_DURATION_SECONDS.with_label_values passes 3 label value(s), the metric declares 2; aborts on a mismatch (prometheus unwraps inside the dependency)

        1 problem(s): 83 registrations, 155 calls checked
        exit=1

    $ python3 scripts/ci/panic-site-census.py --check-baseline scripts/ci/panic-site-baseline.tsv
        baseline holds: 97 crates, 0 fewer production sites than recorded
        exit=0

    $ cargo check -p xai-grok-workspace --lib --message-format short
        Finished `dev` profile [unoptimized] target(s) in 19.07s
        exit=0

    $ cargo test -p xai-grok-workspace --lib -- startup_stage_observe_records_independent_samples
        running 1 test
        test handle::tests::startup_stage_observe_records_independent_samples ... FAILED
        ---- handle::tests::startup_stage_observe_records_independent_samples stdout ----
        thread 'handle::tests::startup_stage_observe_records_independent_samples' (3977445) panicked at ~/.cargo/registry/src/*/prometheus-0.14.0/src/vec.rs:296:49:
        called `Result::unwrap()` on an `Err` value: InconsistentCardinality { expect: 2, got: 3 }
        test result: FAILED. 0 passed; 1 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 1934 filtered out; finished in 0.00s
        exit=101

The census is green because there is no panic token at the call site to count; the panic is
in the dependency. `cargo check` is green because `&[&str; 3]` coerces to the `&[V]` the
method takes, whatever the metric was registered with. The test run is the honest part: the
abort is not theoretical, it is `InconsistentCardinality { expect: 2, got: 3 }` on the line
section 0 quotes, and it is the only one of the four commands that noticed.

That test is also the limit of the honest claim. This particular call is reached by
`startup_stage_observe_records_independent_samples`, so for this one site the crate's own
suite does catch the mistake, on the legs that run it. Nothing equivalent covers the drain,
recovery, swap and OOM calls in section 1, and the abort there happens in a process someone
is waiting on, which is why the finding is worth having before any code runs.

== 3. The mutation matrix

Each row is one `write_text` on `crates/codegen/xai-grok-workspace/src/handle.rs`, then the
gate, then a restore. The driver refuses a mutation whose target text is not unique, so a
row that would change two places reports `SKIPPED` instead of silently changing both.

    M1  one label value too many at a real call site
        handle.rs:85: arity: ... passes 3 label value(s), the metric declares 2
        1 problem(s)                                          expected: arity    ok
    M2  a declared label dropped from a real registration
        six findings: the two production calls plus four in handle_tests.rs, all
        "passes 2 label value(s), the metric declares 1"       expected: arity    ok
    M3  a metric name that duplicates another one
        handle.rs:20: duplicate-name: 'grok_workspace_drain_started_total' is registered
        2 times (handle.rs:20, handle.rs:28)                   expected: name     ok
    M4  a metric name Prometheus would reject
        handle.rs:44: bad-name: 'grok-workspace-drain-lost-items_total' is not a
        Prometheus metric name                                 expected: name     ok
    M5  a label name Prometheus would reject
        handle.rs:62: bad-label: grok_workspace_startup_stage_duration_seconds: label
        'outcome count' is not a valid Prometheus label name    expected: label    ok

M2 is the row worth reading twice: one edited line, a registration, turns red at six call
sites in three files, because the gate resolves each call to the metric it names. M3's
message names both registrations, which is the pair a reader has to reconcile; and
`register_int_counter_vec!` for the second one returns `AlreadyRegistered`, whose `unwrap`
aborts the first thread that touches that metric, not the module that registered it.

== 4. The tree afterwards

Every restore is a copy followed by a byte comparison, not an `git checkout` that could also
revert something else:

    $ cp {SCRATCH}/handle.rs.orig crates/codegen/xai-grok-workspace/src/handle.rs
    $ cmp {SCRATCH}/handle.rs.orig crates/codegen/xai-grok-workspace/src/handle.rs
        (no output)

    restored byte-identically: True     (M1)
    restored byte-identically: True     (M2)
    restored byte-identically: True     (M3)
    restored byte-identically: True     (M4)
    restored byte-identically: True     (M5)

    == final tree state: identical to backup: True
       git status for that file: (clean)

The copy preserves the bytes and deliberately not the mtime: the artifact left in `target/`
after section 2 was built from the mutated file, and restoring an older mtime would let cargo
call that artifact fresh. `shutil.copy` leaves the timestamp at "now", so the next cargo run
rebuilds from the restored source instead of testing the mutated binary.

== 5. Fixtures, and the bug the real tree caught first

    $ python3 scripts/ci/test-check-metric-labels.py
        Ran 33 tests in 22.572s

        OK

The suite drives the shipped script over synthetic trees and pins each decision from both
sides where one side alone would not prove anything: a call with one value too many is paired
with the same metric called correctly, so a scanner that stopped matching `with_label_values`
cannot pass by reporting nothing. One case runs the gate on this repository with the
committed ledger, so it cannot be green in fixtures and red where it was written.

Writing the gate against the real tree found two bugs that no fixture had suggested. The
element counter started counting depth inside the opening bracket, so no top-level comma ever
split and it reported 70 arity problems where the fixed counter finds none; the argument
scanner tracked only parentheses and ignored string literals, so `&[]` parsed as `]` and a `)`
inside a help string could close the argument list early. Both are pinned now: one case uses
the one-entry-per-line trailing comma most label arrays in this repository use, one passes an
empty label list, one puts an unbalanced `)` in a help string.

== 6. Wiring

The gate is in both places a guard has to be, with the same command line, which is what
`check-guard-wiring.py` compares:

    $ grep -n 'check-metric-labels' .github/workflows/ci.yml scripts/verify-in-docker.sh
        .github/workflows/ci.yml:725:          python3 scripts/ci/test-check-metric-labels.py
        .github/workflows/ci.yml:726:          python3 scripts/ci/check-metric-labels.py
        scripts/verify-in-docker.sh:165:  "metric labels: python3 scripts/ci/test-check-metric-labels.py && python3 scripts/ci/check-metric-labels.py"

    $ python3 scripts/ci/check-guard-wiring.py
        check-guard-wiring: OK (55 files in scripts/ci/, 54 reachable, 50 run by scripts/verify-in-docker.sh, 4 recorded CI-only, 1 exempt)

== 7. The whole host gate sweep afterwards

    $ bash scripts/verify-gates.sh
        PASS  panic-site census
        PASS  metric labels
        ...
        SKIP  cargo check (build gate; --with-build runs it)
        SKIP  cargo clippy (build gate; --with-build runs it)
        SKIP  cargo test (build gate; --with-build runs it)
        SKIP  GUI protocol types (build gate; --with-build runs it)

        all gates passed on the host (29 run, 4 skipped)

29 gates run where the previous commit ran 28; the four skips are the same four build gates
the host runner has always skipped, and the container runs them. Whole sweep 202 seconds.
Section 2 is the reason the `PASS  metric labels` line is worth a gate: it is the only
automatic check in the repository, other than a test that happens to reach the line, that
would have printed anything about a call with one label value too many.
